<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charminar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1591">1591</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Charminar is a monument and mosque in the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, built in 1591 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-quli-qutb-shah">Muhammad Quli Qutb Shah</a> of the Golconda Sultanate when he founded the city. Its square body is crowned at each corner by a minaret about 56 metres high, which gives the building its name, 'four minarets'. It is the best-known symbol of Hyderabad.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charminar.jpg" alt="Charminar" width="320" height="452" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DidierTais · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charminar_Hyderabad_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Construction began in 1589 and was completed in two years, at a cost of 9 lakh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a>, about 2 lakh gold huns. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-quli-qutb-shah">Muhammad Quli Qutb Shah</a>, the fifth ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutb-shahi-dynasty">Qutb Shahi dynasty</a>, built it after moving his capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a> to the newly founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a>. Accounts of its purpose differ. The Archaeological Survey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> records that it is widely accepted to have been built at the centre of the city to commemorate the end of a plague. The 17th-century French traveller Jean de Thévenot, whose narration was complemented with Persian texts, says it marked the beginning of the second Islamic millennium, the year 1000 AH.</p><p>The monument stood at the crossing of the trade route that linked the city to the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/machilipatnam">Machilipatnam</a>, and the Old City was laid out around it in four quadrants. Architects from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> were invited to help plan the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The building is in the Indo-Islamic style with Persian elements and was intended as a mosque and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a>. Its square body is 20 metres on each side, and each side opens in a grand arch facing a cardinal direction. The four fluted minarets, each with a double balcony and a bulbous dome, are built into the main structure, unlike the free-standing minarets of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taj-mahal">Taj Mahal</a>. A flight of 149 winding steps leads to the upper floor. The structure is of granite, limestone, mortar and pulverised marble, and is known for its stucco decoration.</p><p>The mosque occupies the top floor of the four-storey building. Its main gallery has 45 covered prayer spaces, and the rest of the roof served as a royal court in Qutb Shahi times. Clocks on the four sides were added in 1889.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>A minaret fell after being struck by lightning in 1670 and was repaired, and in 1820 Sikandar Jah paid for the renovation of part of the building. The Charminar remained the tallest structure in Hyderabad for 396 years, until 1987. To the south-west stands the Makkah Masjid, whose central arch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-quli-qutb-shah">Muhammad Quli Qutb Shah</a> is said to have built with bricks made from soil brought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>. The Laad Bazaar around the monument is known for jewellery, especially bangles, and in its heyday the Charminar market had some 14,000 shops.</p><p>The Charminar appears on the coins and banknotes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a> rupee and on the emblem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a>. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda Fort</a> and the Qutb Shahi Tombs it was submitted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> tentative list on 10 September 2010.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charminar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Muhammad Quli Qutb Shah founded Hyderabad in 1591 and built the <b>Charminar</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/golconda-sultanate">Golconda Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
